**1. No policy that skips KV holds 99% of full-cache accuracy below a 100% budget.** Query-aware
selection comes closest. On {{ phase9.analysis.summary.prompts | int }} paired prompts it fails the
bar at 50% (95% CI {{ phase9.analysis.conditions.quest_50.retention_ci95.0 | pct }}–{{ phase9.analysis.conditions.quest_50.retention_ci95.1 | pct }})
and sits on it at 75% (CI {{ phase9.analysis.conditions.quest_75.retention_ci95.0 | pct }}–{{ phase9.analysis.conditions.quest_75.retention_ci95.1 | pct }}).
Rung 9 clears it by computing the missing attention on the CPU and merging it exactly: the last
percent of accuracy is available, but it costs compute, not memory.

**2. Moving fewer bytes never made decode faster.** At 25% the tier moves
{{ phase4.analysis.summary.fetch_mib_at.tiered_sync.b25 | .1f }} MiB per token, well under a
millisecond of PCIe time, while bringing each layer's top-K indices back to the host costs
{{ phase4.analysis.summary.rank_ms_per_token.tiered_sync.min | .1f }}–{{ phase4.analysis.summary.rank_ms_per_token.tiered_sync.max | .1f }} ms.
Fewer fetches, fully hidden prefetch ({{ phase4.analysis.summary.overlap_fraction_median.min | pct0 }}
overlap) and an int8 tier that halves traffic were all no faster. Decode is bound by the host
deciding what to move.

**3. Taking the host out of the loop fixes it at small budgets.** A zero-copy tier ([Phase 23](docs/phases/PHASE_23.md))
maps pinned memory into the GPU's address space and gathers selected blocks in place, with no
residency table and no sync. At 6.25% it decodes at rung 5's speed
({{ phase23.analysis.summary.speedup_vs_tier.b6_25 | .2f }}× rung 6); at 50% it is
{{ phase23.analysis.summary.speedup_vs_tier.b50 | .2f }}× rung 6, because it rereads every selected
block every step.

